{"cuisine/british-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/british-chinese-cuisine","n":"British Chinese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in United Kingdom","intro":[["British Chinese cuisine is a style of Chinese cuisine developed by British Chinese people in the United Kingdom, typically adapted to British tastes but increasingly inspired by authentic Cantonese dishes. It is considered a major part of British cuisine. It often consists of Sichuan cuisine and Cantonese cuisine with chips and curry sauce available, which are not known for being traditionally Chinese but are food staples in the UK."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"british-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Chinese Takeaway Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Kwoklyn Wan","note":"British Chinese takeaway classics from a chef raised in his family's UK takeaway, published by Quadrille.","url":"https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/467289/chinese-takeaway-cookbook-by-kwoklyn-wan/9781787133679","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781787133679"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Salt and pepper chips","dish/salt-and-pepper-chips"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["French fries","ingredient/french-fries"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/27/Yummy_Kitchen_Chinese_take-away.jpg/500px-Yummy_Kitchen_Chinese_take-away.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Wikociewie","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yummy_Kitchen_Chinese_take-away.jpg"}},"cuisine/padang-food":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/padang-food","n":"Padang food","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Padang cuisine or Minangkabau cuisine is the cuisine of the Minangkabau people of West Sumatra, Indonesia. It is among the most popular cuisines in Maritime Southeast Asia. It is known across Indonesia as masakan Padang after Padang, the capital city of Western Sumatra province. It is served in restaurants mostly owned by perantauan (migrating) Minangkabau people in Indonesian cities. Padang food is ubiquitous in Indonesian cities and is popular in neighboring Malaysia, Southern Philippines, East Timor, Brunei, and Singapore."],["Padang food is famous for its use of coconut milk and spicy chili. Minang cuisine consists of three main elements: gulai (curry), lado (chili pepper) and bareh (rice). Among the cooking traditions in Indonesian cuisine, Minangkabau cuisine and most of Sumatran cuisine are influenced by Indian and Middle Eastern cuisine, with dishes cooked in curry sauce with coconut milk and a wide variety of spice mixes."],["Because most Minangkabau people are Muslims, Minangkabau cuisine follows halal dietary law rigorously. Most of its protein is taken from beef, chicken, water buffalo, goat, lamb, mutton, poultry and fish. Minangkabau people are known for their fondness of cattle meat products including offal. Almost all the parts of cattle are used in Minangkabau dishes. Seafood is popular in coastal West Sumatran cities, and most are grilled or fried with spicy chili sauce or in curry gravy. Fish, shrimp, and cuttlefish are cooked in similar fashion. Most Minangkabau food is eaten with hot steamed rice or compressed rice such as katupek (ketupat). Vegetables are mostly boiled, such as boiled cassava leaf, or simmered in thin curry as side dishes, such as gulai of young jackfruit or cabbage."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"padang-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padang_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Mini Spicy Padang Cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"William W. Wongso","note":"Short guide to Minang dishes by a noted Indonesian chef.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":6,"links":[["Ayam pop","dish/ayam-pop"],["Balado (food)","dish/balado-food"],["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Nasi kapau","dish/nasi-kapau"],["Nasi padang","dish/nasi-padang"],["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Coconut water","ingredient/coconut-water"],["Bird","ingredient/bird"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Bumbu (seasoning)","ingredient/bumbu-seasoning"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Chilli pepper","ingredient/chilli-pepper"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Fennel","ingredient/fennel"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Side dish","ingredient/side-dish"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Coconut cream","ingredient/coconut-cream"],["Garcinia atroviridis","ingredient/garcinia-atroviridis"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Padang food cooking","count":2,"links":[["Palembang cuisine","cuisine/palembang-cuisine"],["Peranakan cuisine","cuisine/peranakan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f6/Nasi_Kapau.JPG/500px-Nasi_Kapau.JPG","w":500,"h":325,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasi_Kapau.JPG"}},"dish/akuri":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/akuri","n":"Akuri","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Akuri is a spicy scrambled egg dish eaten in Parsi cuisine of India. Akuri is cooked until almost runny; the eggs are never overcooked. The main flavouring is fried onions and the spices used are ginger, coriander, chopped chilis, and black pepper. Akuri is traditionally eaten with pav or double roti (types of Indian bread)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-akuri"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akuri"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Scrambled egg","ingredient/scrambled-egg"],["Scrambled eggs","ingredient/scrambled-eggs"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Swasthi's Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"indianhealthyrecipes.com","note":"Swasthi Shreekanth's long-running blog of everyday Indian home cooking from across the regions.","url":"https://www.indianhealthyrecipes.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Hari Ghotra","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"harighotra.co.uk","note":"UK-based cook Hari Ghotra's recipe library and videos of home-style Indian dishes.","url":"https://www.harighotra.co.uk/indian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Unicorns in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"unicornsinthekitchen.com","note":"Iranian-born Shadi HasanzadeNemati's blog with a large Persian recipe section.","url":"https://www.unicornsinthekitchen.com/persian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indian cuisine","links":[["Aloo paratha","dish/aloo-paratha"],["Amba (condiment)","dish/amba-condiment"],["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"],["Bombay potato","dish/bombay-potato"],["Butter chicken","dish/butter-chicken"],["Chaat masala","dish/chaat-masala"],["Chicken Chettinad","dish/chicken-chettinad"],["Dahi vada","dish/dahi-vada"],["Dal dhokli","dish/dal-dhokli"],["Dal makhani","dish/dal-makhani"],["Fafda","dish/fafda"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Handvo","dish/handvo"],["Hyderabadi haleem","dish/hyderabadi-haleem"],["Idli","dish/idli"],["Indian restaurant","dish/indian-restaurant"],["Kachumber","dish/kachumber"],["Kidyo","dish/kidyo"],["Kulfi","dish/kulfi"],["Masala chai","dish/masala-chai"],["Mirchi bada","dish/mirchi-bada"],["Mokthuk","dish/mokthuk"],["Naan","dish/naan"],["Osmania biscuit","dish/osmania-biscuit"],["Panipuri","dish/panipuri"],["Pathia","dish/pathia"],["Rabri","dish/rabri"],["Shemai","dish/shemai"],["Tandoori chicken","dish/tandoori-chicken"],["Tandoori roti","dish/tandoori-roti"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Zarda (food)","dish/zarda-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ad/%D8%AE%D8%A7%DA%AF%DB%8C%D9%86%D9%87_%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%81%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86%DB%8C.jpg/500px-%D8%AE%D8%A7%DA%AF%DB%8C%D9%86%D9%87_%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%81%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86%DB%8C.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Behnam N","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%AE%D8%A7%DA%AF%DB%8C%D9%86%D9%87_%D8%A7%D8%B5%D9%81%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86%DB%8C.jpg"}},"ingredient/mandarin-orange":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mandarin-orange","n":"Mandarin orange","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A mandarin orange (Citrus reticulata), often simply called mandarin, is a small, rounded citrus tree fruit, which is usually eaten plain or in fruit salads. The mandarin is small and oblate, unlike the roughly spherical sweet orange (which is a mandarin-pomelo hybrid). The taste is sweeter and stronger than the common orange. A ripe mandarin orange is firm to slightly soft, heavy for its size, and pebbly-skinned. The peel is thin and loose, with little white mesocarp, so they are usually easier to peel and to split into segments. Hybrids have these traits to lesser degrees. The mandarin orange is tender and is damaged easily by cold. It can be grown in tropical and subtropical areas."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mandarin-orange"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandarin_orange"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states",[["Ambrosia (fruit salad)","dish/ambrosia-fruit-salad"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Marshmallow","ingredient/marshmallow"],["Pineapple","ingredient/pineapple"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/10/Citrus_reticulata_April_2013_Nordbaden.JPG/500px-Citrus_reticulata_April_2013_Nordbaden.JPG","w":500,"h":667,"by":"4028mdk09","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citrus_reticulata_April_2013_Nordbaden.JPG"}},"dish/ayam-pop":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ayam-pop","n":"Ayam pop","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Padang food","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ayam pop is a fried chicken dish originating from Minangkabau cuisine commonly found in Indonesia, consisting of chicken deep fried in oil. The dish contains skinless pale fried chicken that has been boiled or steamed prior to frying. Although ayam pop is identified as ayam goreng (fried chicken), ayam pop is different from common ayam goreng. While fried chicken is golden brown, ayam pop is light-colored."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ayam-pop"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayam_pop"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coconut water","ingredient/coconut-water"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"]]},{"h":"More from Padang food","links":[["Balado (food)","dish/balado-food"],["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Nasi kapau","dish/nasi-kapau"],["Nasi padang","dish/nasi-padang"],["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/da/Ayam_Pop_1.jpg/500px-Ayam_Pop_1.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"S Kartika","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayam_Pop_1.jpg"}},"ingredient/durum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/durum","n":"Durum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Durum (), also called pasta wheat or macaroni wheat (Triticum durum or Triticum turgidum subsp. durum), is a tetraploid species of wheat. It is the second-most cultivated species of wheat after common wheat, although it represents only 5 to 8% of global wheat production. It was developed by artificial selection of the domesticated emmer wheat strains formerly grown in Central Europe and the Near East around 7000 BC, which developed a naked, free-threshing form. Like emmer, durum is awned (with bristles). It is the predominant wheat grown in the Middle East."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-durum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine",[["Busiate","dish/busiate"]]]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5d/Triticum_durum.jpg/500px-Triticum_durum.jpg","w":500,"h":466,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Triticum_durum.jpg"}},"dish/chili-con-carne":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chili-con-carne","n":"Chili con carne","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Chili con carne, often shortened to chili, is a spicy stew of Mexican origin containing chili peppers (sometimes in the form of chili powder), meat (usually beef), tomatoes, and beans. Other seasonings may include garlic, onions, and cumin."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chili-con-carne"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chili_con_carne"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"],["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"],["Tex-Mex cuisine","cuisine/tex-mex-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Mexico in My Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mexicoinmykitchen.com","note":"Mely Martínez's long-running blog of Mexican home-style recipes.","url":"https://www.mexicoinmykitchen.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Pati Jinich","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"patijinich.com","note":"Recipe archive from the Mexican-born chef and host of the PBS series Pati's Mexican Table.","url":"https://patijinich.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Homesick Texan","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"homesicktexan.com","note":"Lisa Fain's long-running blog of Texan and Tex-Mex home cooking.","url":"https://www.homesicktexan.com/homesick-texan-cookbook-introduction/comment-page-20/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chow-chow (food)","dish/chow-chow-food"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/50/Bowl_of_chili.jpg/500px-Bowl_of_chili.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Carstor","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bowl_of_chili.jpg"}},"ingredient/red-wine":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/red-wine","n":"Red wine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Red wine is a type of wine made from dark-colored grape varieties (red grapes). The color of the wine can range from intense violet, typical of young wines, through to brick red for mature wines and brown for older red wines. The juice from most purple grapes is greenish-white, the red color coming from anthocyan pigments present in the skin of the grape. Much of the red wine production process involves extraction of color and flavor components from the grape skin."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-red-wine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_wine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"]]],["Norwegian cuisine","cuisine/norwegian-cuisine",[["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa"]]],["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine",[["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa"]]],["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku",[["Hayashi rice","dish/hayashi-rice"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Demi-glace","ingredient/demi-glace"],["Mushroom","ingredient/mushroom"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/c/c0/Red_Wine_Glass.jpg","w":500,"h":935,"by":"André Karwath aka Aka","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Wine_Glass.jpg"}},"dish/draw-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/draw-soup","n":"Draw soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Yoruba cuisine","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Draw soup is the pidgin english name of some soups in parts of Nigeria that are made from okra, ogbono (Irvingia gabonensis) or ewedu leaves (jute). The name derives from the thick viscosity characteristic of the broth as it draws out of the bowl when eaten either with a spoon or, more characteristically, by dipping a small piece of solid (fufu) into it. It can be served with numerous Nigerian  fufu meals, including eba (garri) and pounded yam."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-draw-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Draw_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Yoruba cuisine","cuisine/yoruba-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"],["Ewedu","ingredient/ewedu"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["Iru","ingredient/iru"],["Ogbono","ingredient/ogbono"],["Okra","ingredient/okra"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"]]},{"h":"More from Yoruba cuisine","links":[["Amala (food)","dish/amala-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8e/Pounded_Yam_and_Okro_Soup.jpg/500px-Pounded_Yam_and_Okro_Soup.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Justdifference","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pounded_Yam_and_Okro_Soup.jpg"}},"dish/fried-okra":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/fried-okra","n":"Fried okra","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Soul food","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Fried okra is a side dish associated with the cuisine of the Southern United States, particularly soul food. It is made by coating sliced okra in cornmeal and then frying it."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-fried-okra"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fried_okra"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Soul food","cuisine/soul-food"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Soul Food Pot","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thesoulfoodpot.com","note":"Shaunda Necole's blog 