"Hellokitty893112 at en.wikipedia","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caf%C3%A9_Sabarsky_Sachertorte.jpg"}},"ingredient/rutabaga":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rutabaga","n":"Rutabaga","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Rutabaga or swede (British English and some Commonwealth English) is a root vegetable, a form of Brassica napus (which also includes rapeseed).  Other names include Swedish turnip, neep (Scots), and turnip (Scottish and Canadian English, Irish English, Cornish English and Manx English, as well as some dialects of English in Northern England, New England English, and Australian English). However, elsewhere, the name turnip usually refers to the related white turnip."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rutabaga"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rutabaga"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Scotch broth","dish/scotch-broth"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Pulse (legume)","ingredient/pulse-legume"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a7/Rutabaga%2C_variety_nadmorska.JPG/500px-Rutabaga%2C_variety_nadmorska.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Picasa user Seedambassadors","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rutabaga%2C_variety_nadmorska.JPG"}},"ingredient/raw-fish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/raw-fish","n":"Raw fish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Raw fish or shellfish dishes include marinated raw fish (soaked in a seasoned liquid) and raw fish which is lightly cured such as gravlax, but not fish which is fully cured (fermented, pickled, smoked or otherwise preserved)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-raw-fish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raw_fish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Sushi","dish/sushi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Nori","ingredient/nori"],["Sumeshi","ingredient/sumeshi"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/68/Herring.jpg/500px-Herring.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Andrew Hayward","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herring.jpg"}},"dish/ta-a-mi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ta-a-mi","n":"Tàⁿ-á-mī","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Taiwan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Tàⁿ-á-mī, also known as ta-a noodles or danzai noodles, are a type of snack found in Tainan, Taiwan. Also known as \"slack season ta-a noodles\", they originated in Tainan (in southern Taiwan) about 130 years ago. While the general recipe is well known, some of the spices and the proportions of the various ingredients (shrimp-flavored soup, shrimp, minced pork, coriander, and garlic) are closely guarded secrets by the different restaurants and stands that serve it."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ta-a-mi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T%C3%A0%E2%81%BF-%C3%A1-m%C4%AB"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Metapenaeopsis barbata","ingredient/metapenaeopsis-barbata"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Cooking in Chinglish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookinginchinglish.com","note":"Taiwanese home recipes from Edwina, who grew up in Taiwan.","url":"https://cookinginchinglish.com","suggested":true,"kind":"b